---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import Opening from '../../components/Opening.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
const SPACE = [2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 40, 48, 64, 80, 96, 128];
---

<Shell section="foundations" title="Spacing" description="A 4px and 8px grid, with 2px as one named exception.">
  <h1 class="display">Spacing</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A 4px and 8px grid, with 2px kept as one deliberate exception. Naming that exception is better than pretending it does
    not exist and leaving people to hardcode it.
  </p>

  <Opening kind="spacing" />

  <h2>Why 2px is in the scale</h2>

  <p>
    Some gaps genuinely are smaller than 4px: a status dot next to its label, the space between a field and its error
    message, the inset of a focus ring. A system that bans those gaps does not remove them, it just moves them into
    inline styles where nobody can see them. So 2px is in the scale, documented as the exception, and everything else is a
    multiple of 4.
  </p>

  <Demo padded>
    <div style="inline-size: 100%; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--spacing-space-8);">
      {SPACE.map((n) => (
        <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: var(--spacing-space-12);">
          <code style="inline-size: 120px; flex: none; font-size: var(--typography-text-xs-size);">space-{n}</code>
          <span style={`inline-size: var(--spacing-space-${n}); block-size: var(--spacing-space-16); background: var(--sem-primary-bg); border-radius: var(--brand-radius-scale-xs-2);`}></span>
          <span style="font-size: var(--typography-text-xs-size); color: var(--sem-fg-tertiary);">{n}px</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Demo>

  <h2>Which step for which gap</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Token</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>space-2</code></td><td>The exception. A dot to its label, a field to its error line</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-4</code></td><td>Inside a tight control: icon to label in a small button</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-8</code></td><td>Between related controls: two buttons in a row, a checkbox and its label</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-12</code></td><td>Inside a card or a row of a table</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-16</code></td><td>Card padding, between fields in a form</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-24</code></td><td>Between groups of fields, section padding</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>space-32</code> and up</td><td>Between sections of a page</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The rule that matters most</h2>

  <p>
    <strong>Space belongs to the container, not to the child.</strong> A card decides the gaps between its children; a button
    does not add a margin hoping for the best. In practice that means reaching for <code>gap</code> on a flex or grid parent
    rather than <code>margin</code> on the things inside it, which is why almost nothing in ERA sets a margin.
  </p>

  <p>
    The payoff is that a component can be dropped into any layout without bringing its own spacing opinions, and two
    components side by side never produce a gap neither of them intended.
  </p>
</Shell>
